2. Skeletal adaptations: The skeletal structure of pterosaurs was also highly specialized to enable flight. The bones of the body were lightweight and hollow, which reduced the overall weight of the animal. The bones were also reinforced with internal structures, such as struts and trabeculae, which provided strength and stability. The tail of pterosaurs was short and pointed, which helped to reduce drag and improve maneuverability. The legs of pterosaurs were relatively short and slender, which allowed them to walk and run on the ground. The feet of pterosaurs were adapted for grasping and climbing, which allowed them to perch on trees and other structures.

3. Other adaptations: In addition to the unique wing structure and skeletal structure, pterosaurs also had several other adaptations that enabled them to fly. These adaptations included a large keel on the ulna, which provided a strong attachment point for the wing membrane, and a long, pointed tail, which helped to reduce drag and improve maneuverability. Pterosaurs also had a large number of feathers, which provided lift and stability during flight.

