How does chronic stress affect the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system and what is the underlying mechanism responsible for this effect?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
How does chronic stress affect the functionality of natural killer cells in the immune system and what is the underlying mechanism responsible for this effect?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Chronic stress can impair the function of natural killer (NK) cells in the immune system. NK cells are a type of white blood cell that play a crucial role in the immune system by recognizing and killing abnormal cells, such as virus-infected or tumor cells. They are part of the innate immune system, which is the first line of defense against pathogens and abnormal cells.

When stress becomes chronic, it can lead to a significant reduction in NK cell function. This can result in a weakened immune response, making individuals more susceptible to infections and potentially increasing the risk of cancer development.

NK cells are activated by a balance of activating and inhibitory signals from the surface of target cells. They also rely on the surrounding microenvironment for proper function. Chronic stress disrupts this balance, leading to impaired NK cell activity.

