2. **Saltory conduction**: In myelinated nerve fibers, the membrane is separated into regions of high resistance (myelin) and low resistance (nodes of Ranvier). The action potential ""jumps"" from one node of Ranvier to the next, bypassing the myelinated regions. This allows the action potential to travel much faster because the membrane potential is only restored at the nodes of Ranvest.  

