\]  
where \( \beta \) is the damping coefficient, \( \dot{y} \) is the velocity, and \( \omega_0 \) is the natural frequency.  

This equation is a first-order approximation to the true motion, which includes the effects of friction or resistance. The damping coefficient \( \beta \) depends on the properties of the system (e.g., mass, viscosity, and speed of motion).  

The solution to this equation depends on the value of \( \beta \):  
- If \( \beta^2 < 4\omega_0^2 \), the system is underdamped.  
- If \( \beta^2 = 4\omega_0^2 \), the system is critically damped.  
- If \( \beta^2 > 4\omega_0^2 \), the system is overdamped.  

