","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law: \( a = \frac{F}{m} \), where \( F \) is the force and \( m \) is the mass.  
The electric force on the proton is given by \( F = qE \), where \( q \) is the charge and \( E \) is the electric field strength.  
Substituting \( F = qE \) into \( a = \frac{F}{m} \), we get \( a = \frac{qE}{m} \).  
Substituting the given values:  
\[ a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = \frac{800 \times 10^{-20} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 480.06 \, \text{m/s}^2 \).  
The magnitude of the acceleration is approximately \( 480 \, \text{m/s}^2 \).  

(b) The proton starts from rest, so \( v_0 = 0 \).  
Using the kinematic equation \( v = v_0 + a t \), where \( v \) is the final velocity, \( v_0 \) is the initial velocity, \( a \) is the acceleration, and \( t \) is the time, we can solve for \( t \):  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{480.06 \, \text{m/s}^2} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{480.06 \, \text{m/s}^2} \approx 74.1 \, \text{s} \).  

Final Answer:
(a) \(\boxed{480 \, \text{m/s}^2}\)  
