The tangential acceleration \( a_t \) is given as 2 m s⁻², and the radius \( r \) is 0.5 m. The tangential acceleration is related to the angular acceleration \( \alpha \) by \( a_t = r \alpha \).  
Thus, \( \alpha = \frac{a_t}{r} = \frac{2 \text{ m/s}^2}{0.5 \text{ m}} = 4 \text{ rad/s}^2 \).  

The net force \( F_t \) acting on the pulley is given by \( F_t = I \alpha \), where \( I \) is the moment of inertia of the disc. For a disc, \( I = \frac{1}{2} m r^2 \), where \( m \) is the mass and \( r \) is the radius.  
