- \( \rho = 1000 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \) (density of water)
- \( g = 9.81 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)
- \( h = 5 \, \text{m} \) (depth of the inlet pipe)

\[
P_{hydrostatic} = 1000 \times 9.81 \times 5 = 49,550 \, \text{Pa} = 49.55 \, \text{kPa}
\]

**Step 3: Determine the saturation vapor pressure at 20°C**
The saturation vapor pressure is the pressure at which water will vaporize at a given temperature. For 20°C, the saturation vapor pressure is approximately 10,000 Pa (or 10 kPa).

**Step 4: Determine the minimum inlet pressure to prevent cavitation**
To prevent cavitation, the inlet pressure must be greater than the saturation vapor pressure at the pump inlet.
\[
P_{min} > P_{sat}
\]
