","To find the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil, we can use the formula for the magnetic field at the center of a circular coil:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2R}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil.
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free matter, which is \( 4 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T m/A} \).
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil.
- \( I \) is the current flowing through the coil.
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil.

Given:
- \( N = 100 \) turns.
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \).
- \( R = 0.2 \, \text{m} \).

Substituting the given values into the formula:

\[
B = \frac{(4 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T m/A}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}} = \frac{4 \times 10^{-6} \times 100}{0.4} \, \text{T} = \frac{400 \times 10^{-6}}{0.4} \, \text{T} = 1000 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T} = 1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the circular coil is \( 1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T} \).

Final Answer:
\[
\boxed{1 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{T}}
\]",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
