- Acceleration due to gravity, \( g = 9 \, \text{m/s}^2 \)  
- Gravitational force, \( F = 3600 \, \text{N} \)  

First, calculate the mass \( m \) of the satellite using the gravitational force:  
\[
F = m \cdot g \implies m = \frac{F}{g} = \frac{3600}{9} = 400 \, \text{kg}
\]

Next, calculate the kinetic energy \( KE \) of the satellite:  
\[
KE = \frac{1}{2} m v^2 = \frac{1}{2} \times 400 \times (800)^2 = 128 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} = 128 \, \text{MJ}
\]

The kinetic energy of the satellite is \(\boxed{128 \, \text{MJ}}\).
