","For an isothermal process, the work done by the gas is given by the formula:
\[
W = -nR \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]
where:
- \( W \) is the work done by the gas
- \( n \) is the number of moles of the gas
- \( R \) is the ideal gas constant
- \( V_1 \) is the initial volume
- \( V_2 \) is the final volume

Given:
- \( n = 2 \, \text{mol} \)
- \( P_1 = 2 \, \text{atm} \)
- \( V_1 = 4 \, \text{L} \)
- \( V_2 = 8 \, \text{L} \)
- The gas is an ideal gas, so \( R = 8.31 \, \text{J/mol·K} \) (or \( R = 0.082 \, \text{J/mol·atm} \))

Since the process is isothermal, the temperature remains constant.

Using \( R = 8.31 \, \text{J/mol·K} \):
\[
W = - (2 \, \text{mol}) \times (8.31 \, \text{J/mol·K}) \ln \left( \frac{8 \, \text{L}}{4 \, \text{L}} \right) = - 16.62 \, \text{J} \ln (2)
\]
\[
W = - 16.62 \, \text{J} \times 0.6934 = -12.2 \, \text{J}
\]

